  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nCurrently, an upper ridge is centered to our east with over 1,030mb \nsurface high pressure in New England. This pattern will promote \ncontinued dry weather and highs in the 70s after a cool morning. \nDaytime mixing will also allow for RH values to drop into the 30s \nbut with a weak MSLP gradient and low-level flow limiting winds. By \nThursday, the ridge becomes suppressed with high pressure remaining \nacross the eastern U.S. Overall, the pattern will remain the same \nwith overall seasonal temperatures and similarly low RH values. \nHeading into Friday, a shortwave will eject out of the Rockies, \nleading to increasing ridging over the region. Height rises will \nlikely allow for temperatures to rise further into the 70s than \nrecent days. \n\nFor Halloween, the shortwave will approach from the west with \nsurface high pressure strengthening and progressing off to our \nnortheast. It is then expected to deepen overnight before reaching \nthe area on Sunday, bringing rainfall by early morning. Exact timing \nof onset remains uncertain, but the best chances for the evening of \nHalloween are in southeast Tennessee and the Plateau. Strong winds \nare also possible in the mountains as guidance suggests \nsoutheasterly 850mb flow over 40 kts with a strong MSLP gradient due \nto the high to the northeast. This will limit rainfall totals \nfurther east, but rainfall is needed due to ongoing drought. After \nthe shortwave progresses east on Monday, drier weather will return \nbefore another potential system by mid-week.",
